 @Trusteft Note that I said clear choice "to me" over the RTX 4070. I do think you're right that people who push their GPU heavily and pay exorbitant electricity prices could benefit from the RTX 4070's more efficient node and architecture. However, the power usage in gaming is certainly not 100w and was noted as a 56w delta in the video. In my case, electricity is fairly cheap, and I've found that a trivial undervolt/power limit generally reduces my power usage in gaming by about 15-22% with only a 3-6% drop in FPS on both AMD and NVIDIA GPUs from the PCs I've had over the years.

Especially with Starfield premium ($100) being included with the 7800 XT, the 7800 XT is clearly the popular choice throughout the US as it is currently fully sold out on our major retailer, Newegg. On the flip side, the RTX 4070 and RX 7700 XT are much less exciting so they're readily available.

Btw there's another long-term issue here for me. If upgrading, I'd also pick up a nice 1440p ultrawide monitor as I'm already used to ultrawide and enjoy the immersion. The 12GB of VRAM for that resolution would be just acceptable. My 8GB GPUs have been stuttering when cranking up textures with recent releases at a modest 1080p ultrawide due to VRAM spillover. The additional VRAM isn't too much of a deal today, but would also be a factor in the long-term cost as it'd become a bottleneck sooner.

